  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Yellow Foot (Craterellus lutescens) is a small, trumpet-shaped fungus with a distinctive golden-yellow color and false gills (ridges) running down its stem. Despite its delicate appearance, it is a choice edible mushroom prized by foragers in New England and throughout North America.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "False Gills",
      "description": "Ridges running down the stem are blunt, forked, and run unevenly—not true blade-like gills",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Golden-Yellow Color",
      "description": "Distinctive bright yellow to orange-yellow coloration on both the cap and stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Hollow Stem",
      "description": "The fruiting body is completely hollow from cap to base when cut vertically",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Yellow Feet fruit in moist, mossy areas of mixed hardwood and coniferous forests, typically appearing from late summer through fall in New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called a chanterelle by some foragers, Yellow Feet are actually classified in a different family (Hydnaceae) and have ridges rather than true gills, making them technically 'false chanterelles.'",
